+# Keep sessions in signed cookies
+
+For whoever changes login, deploys or session storage. Sessions live in signed cookies because expired database
+sessions filled the disk and stopped Saturday pickup.
+
+## Context
+
+In 2023 larder moved from one server to two behind a load balancer, and sessions stayed in each server's memory.
+Members were logged out whenever a request reached the other server, and every deploy logged out everyone; the pantry
+volunteers stopped deploying during opening hours. Sessions moved to Postgres, which survived deploys without adding
+a service to run.
+
+On August 3, the database disk filled up: the session table held 2.1 million rows, with every login since 2023 still
+present because expired sessions were never deleted. Larder was down from 09:40 to 12:50 during Saturday pickup.
+Volunteers used a paper list, and eleven people left without their pantry order because volunteers could not find
+it. Deleting expired rows restored service. [Issue 63](../../notes/issue-63.md) records the discussion and the
+August 9 shipment of the replacement.
+
+A deploy must still log no one out. The volunteers want fewer services and maintenance jobs to watch, and session
+storage must no longer grow unchecked. They accept losing individual server-side revocation within the
+[session lifetime](../features/sessions.md#lifetime).
+
+## Options
+
+- **Sticky sessions at the load balancer**: no code change; lost because a deploy still logs out everyone on the
+  restarted server.
+- **Redis as a session store**: fast and common; lost because it adds a service the volunteers would run only for
+  sessions.
+- **Postgres with a nightly expiry cleanup job**: Postgres was chosen first because it survived deploys and was
+  already running. Cleanup would prevent a repeat of the disk incident, but adds a job to watch and keeps a database
+  read on every request.
+- **Signed cookies**: chosen; remove the session table and its per-request query without adding a service or cleanup
+  job, at the cost of individual server-side revocation.
+
+## Decision
+
+Keep the member and expiry in a signed cookie that every web server can verify with the same key. This survives
+deploys while removing session storage, queries and cleanup work. The accepted revocation trade-off is reasonable
+for a pantry with the current session lifetime. The [session implementation](../../larder/sessions.py) supplies the
+signing and verification; the [architecture](../architecture.md#how-they-connect) shows how it connects.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The session table is dropped, and session checks no longer depend on Postgres. Deploys preserve logins when servers
+share the key. Maintainers must account for the [revocation limit and key rotation policy](../features/sessions.md#known-limits).
